WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.420
Speaker 1: Let's start today with the economy.&nbsp;
Is a massive recession coming? It's question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:14.940
one. Question two is. If so or if not, what,&nbsp;
if anything, should you or I or anybody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:21.660
be doing about it? We're seeing increasingly&nbsp;
hyperbolic predictions about just a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:28.800
global and American recession coming. Is one&nbsp;
coming? I have no idea. If you're waiting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.620
me to give my opinion, I have absolutely no idea&nbsp;
whether a massive recession is coming. But really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.360
neither does anybody else. So let's talk through&nbsp;
it and explain. First of all, as a general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.880
reality, humans aren't good at making&nbsp;
economic predictions, which are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:52.200
not good. And secondly, when there are so many&nbsp;
predictions floating around, someone is predicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:58.260
everything. Somebody is bound to be right just by&nbsp;
chance. So you've got all the people predicting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.640
recession in a week and then in a month and then&nbsp;
in six months and over the next 12 to 18 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.440
over the next two years or no recession, it's&nbsp;
all going to be great. Somebody is bound to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.700
it right just by chance and by the fact&nbsp;
that we have 330 million people in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.200
United States, many of whom are making economic&nbsp;
predictions. That's number two. Number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:25.140
predicting doom and gloom and recessions generally&nbsp;
gets more clicks and gets you invited onto more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.820
television programs and gets more articles&nbsp;
written about your predictions. So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:37.140
important to understand the bias to sensationalism&nbsp;
and zooming out and saying, hey, you know what?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.140
I don't know what's going to happen over&nbsp;
the next two years, but over the next 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.320
the market will probably return an average of&nbsp;
7% per year like it always has. That's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.460
particularly titillating and exciting prediction,&nbsp;
and that's not going to get you booked on too many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.440
television programs. So that's another important&nbsp;
thing to understand the bias to sensationalism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.720
a bias to negative predictions. And then number&nbsp;
four, and this may be the most important thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:10.440
unless something changes with the way our&nbsp;
economy works, there's always a recession coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.140
eventually. So if you predict a recession for&nbsp;
long enough or over a long enough period of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.540
at some point you're going to be right.&nbsp;
Because unless something changes and we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.680
no more recessions. And again, as we've talked&nbsp;
about before, recessions are sort of part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.820
a capitalist economy. If you keep predicting&nbsp;
recessions, at some point you're going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:35.880
right. Now, let's get into the specifics. There&nbsp;
are people who have been predicting a recession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:44.640
a massive recession since 2012, since 2014, since&nbsp;
2016. And the truth is that the economy has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.100
pretty good for most of that time. We had this&nbsp;
COVID collapse, which lasted like six weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:56.220
markets collapsing, unemployment rate increasing,&nbsp;
and then it recovered relatively quickly. They've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.920
been wrong. But if they keep predicting it,&nbsp;
at some point, they might end up being right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:11.220
So now let's get to a question about numbers and&nbsp;
data. Are there good or accurate predictors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.320
when a recession is coming? If we look back in&nbsp;
history and the answer is there are a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:23.760
of indicators that do indeed seem to point&nbsp;
to the economy being, quote, good or, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.680
bad. And these are terms that don't really mean&nbsp;
anything unless we define them a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.680
better. So what can we say about the indicators?&nbsp;
The first thing is, when you start seeing a steady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:42.180
rise in job losses and a surge in the unemployment&nbsp;
rate, that can be a good indicator of a coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:50.040
recession. Now, we don't have that at this point&nbsp;
in time. If we look at job creation, you see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.500
it actually looks pretty good. You see, if you're&nbsp;
looking at the screen here, on the left side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.900
your screen is the unemployment rate. And on&nbsp;
the right side of the screen is job creation. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:08.220
had a big spike in the unemployment rate during&nbsp;
the beginning of COVID and we had a big drop in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.700
payrolls at the at that exact same time. And&nbsp;
then since then, we've seen the unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:21.360
rate go down and remain pretty low and we've&nbsp;
seen payrolls increase and remain, quite frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:30.540
quite high. Now, is it possible is it possible&nbsp;
that you would see a recession despite a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.240
employment situation? Anything is possible, but&nbsp;
it's not the way it tends to go historically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:43.980
I think I had one other chart here. Yes. So this&nbsp;
is just a little bit of a more zoomed in look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.660
the unemployment rate. And as you can see, if&nbsp;
you go back a long way, there's been sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:54.720
like this range and we're well within the range&nbsp;
that we've seen for many, many decades at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.860
point in time. Okay. So what other indicators&nbsp;
could we look at that might signal? All that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.820
a recession is coming while another one is&nbsp;
a decline in GDP. And when you look at GDP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:11.760
you do see that in that start of 2020, there's&nbsp;
this little decline in it. And it's little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:20.340
historically speaking, but it was significant at&nbsp;
the time. And you see that real GDP went down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.380
then it has gone relatively back to normal and&nbsp;
increased. And there were a couple little dips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:32.580
there, but GDP really looks pretty unremarkable.&nbsp;
So that's not an indicator historically that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:39.000
a recession is coming. Another indicator that&nbsp;
historically has been sometimes predictive of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:46.680
a recession is retail sales. And when you look at&nbsp;
retail sales and in fact, we can look at it maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.900
over a 25 year period or even a ten year period,&nbsp;
you see that retail sales are actually pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.880
okay year over year. There was this big decline.&nbsp;
Again, there's so much of this mirroring what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.280
going on with COVID, very large decline in retail&nbsp;
sales at the beginning of 2020 March-April. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:07.200
then there was this very big increase. Looks&nbsp;
like roughly what exactly was that? That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:15.600
sort of like middle of 2021. And retail sales&nbsp;
are pretty unremarkable. So we're just not seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.600
the indicators that are normally predictive of a&nbsp;
recession signal that a recession is coming. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:28.320
what are the differences? The differences&nbsp;
are. Inflation, although it is declining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:36.120
and I believe will continue to decline, has been&nbsp;
high, historically high for about a year or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:42.960
a little bit more. Could that alone, even in the&nbsp;
context of all of these other indicators looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.740
fine, could that maybe point to a recession?&nbsp;
Maybe. But again, it's if things are changing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:56.580
if the circumstances that point to a recession are&nbsp;
different now than they were in the past. So we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:03.000
we're going to have to wait and see. Now, a really&nbsp;
important thing to think about at the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.640
level is what should you be doing? What should&nbsp;
I be doing? What should individuals be doing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.480
If we believe a recession is coming to be best&nbsp;
prepared there really the things that we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:21.180
be doing all the time. So first and foremost, do&nbsp;
you have an emergency fund? When we think about an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:28.560
emergency fund, we're usually talking about $1,000&nbsp;
so that if an unexpected expense above and beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.320
your normal monthly expenses comes up, you don't&nbsp;
have to put it on a high interest credit card or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.600
borrow money. Thousand dollar emergency fund if&nbsp;
you don't have it. In particular, if you think a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:46.320
recession is coming, you should work to establish&nbsp;
that number to understand your health insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:53.100
limits so that, you know, if I have unexpected&nbsp;
medical bills and medical bills are often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:58.860
unexpected. What is the most that I might have to&nbsp;
go out of pocket? So this means understanding your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:04.860
deductible and understanding your maximum out of&nbsp;
pocket. Number three, paying down high interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:13.620
debt for two reasons. Number one, paying 15&nbsp;
20% interest on a credit card is a very bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.520
financial aid. That's an extraordinarily high&nbsp;
interest rate. And if you can avoid paying that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:23.460
you want to avoid paying that. And number two, on&nbsp;
the high interest debt, if you believe a recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:29.520
is or may be coming, and if you want to have as&nbsp;
much financial flexibility as possible, you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.280
to have credit available on those credit cards.&nbsp;
And every dollar you can pay off frees up a dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.680
if indeed you had no choice but to start charging&nbsp;
things in a future recession. And the number four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.740
try to establish that 4 to 6 month reserve&nbsp;
of monthly expenses. So in an ideal world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:53.220
you would figure out what are my monthly expenses?&nbsp;
You would multiply by four or ideally six, or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:58.140
you want to be, you know, have an extra cushion.&nbsp;
And it's not possible for many people buy nine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:03.900
have somewhere between four and six or even nine&nbsp;
months of expenses in the bank. So if your income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:10.440
went to zero for that period of time, you would&nbsp;
still be able to pay your monthly expenses. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.180
is the type of stuff we typically talk about on&nbsp;
the Pacman Finance Channel. So if any of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.800
interesting, we have this whole other channel,&nbsp;
you can find it at YouTube.com, slash, Pacman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.240
finance. And if you go through the topics&nbsp;
we've covered, you see that most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:30.180
things I've mentioned are listed as individual&nbsp;
videos, paying off debt, building a budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:37.560
etc., etc. So check that out at YouTube.com&nbsp;
slash Pacman finance if it is interesting to you

